  "turn": {
    "pericope": "Matthew 13:1-9, 18-23",
    "refKey": "Matt.13.1-Matt.13.23",
    "gravity": "Isaiah promises a word that goes out like rain and does not return empty; the Psalm soaks the furrows and crowns the year with bounty; and Jesus flings seed across every kind of ground. The nerve: the Word is already doing its work — the open question is the ground it lands on, and you are the ground.",
    "trap": "It turns into a scolding to \"be good soil\" — as if dirt could improve itself — which is both impossible and tone-deaf to the wild generosity of the sowing. Or it becomes pure reassurance (\"the Word never fails\") that quietly ignores the three-quarters that bore nothing.",
    "hinge": "The seed is flung with abandon on ground that cannot, by itself, change what it is — and still the call is to hear and bear fruit. The Sower's recklessness and the soil's responsibility both stand; drop either and the parable goes flat.",
    "doors": [
      {
        "landing": "Which ground are you",
        "claim": "The four soils are a mirror held up to the hearer — the trodden path, the shallow rock, the choking thorns, the open field — and the sermon asks which you are today.",
        "witnesses": [
          {
            "author": "Rabanus Maurus",
            "tradition": "medieval",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "The wayside is the mind trodden and hardened by the continual passage of evil thoughts, so that the seed cannot sink in."
          },
          {
            "author": "Jerome",
            "tradition": "latin-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "The deceitfulness of riches chokes the word, for riches are treacherous — promising one thing and doing another."
          }
        ],
        "say": "The same seed fell on all four grounds; the difference was the soil — and the soil is you.",
        "do": "Have each hearer name which ground their heart is this season, and the one thing trodding, drying, or choking the word in them."
      },
      {
        "landing": "The reckless Sower",
        "claim": "No careful farmer sows on a footpath, on rock, in thorns — but this Sower scatters everywhere, and that abandon is the gospel: God writes off no ground.",
        "witnesses": [
          {
            "author": "John Chrysostom",
            "tradition": "greek-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "How is it reasonable to sow among thorns, on stony ground, by the wayside? In ordinary soil it would be folly — but the Sower casts the seed even there."
          },
          {
            "author": "Jerome",
            "tradition": "latin-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "By this Sower is meant the Son of God, who sows among the people the word of the Father."
          }
        ],
        "say": "Look how prodigally God spends the seed — on the hardened, the shallow, the crowded — refusing to decide in advance which ground is hopeless.",
        "do": "Turn them from writing off a hard \"soil\" in their life — a person, a place — to scattering the word there anyway."
      },
      {
        "landing": "Hearing that bears fruit",
        "claim": "Good soil is not just receptive but fruitful — it hears, understands, and yields; a faith that takes the word in and grows nothing is not yet the good ground.",
        "witnesses": [
          {
            "author": "Remigius of Auxerre",
            "tradition": "medieval",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "These ears to hear are the ears of the mind — to understand, and to do the things that are commanded."
          },
          {
            "author": "John Wesley",
            "tradition": "wesleyan",
            "work": "Explanatory Notes",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "The good ground is the one who hears the word and considers it — in spite of Satan; it has deep root, and brings forth fruit in various proportions, some a hundredfold."
          }
        ],
        "say": "The test of the word in you is not how warmly you received it but what it has grown.",
        "do": "Send them to one concrete fruit the word is asking of them this month — and to tend it, not just admire the seed."
      }
    ],
    "synthesis": {
      "convergence": "Three of the readings sing of a Word and Spirit doing fertile, unstoppable work — rain that does not return empty (Isaiah 55), furrows soaked and the year crowned (Psalm 65), the Spirit giving life and freedom (Romans 8) — and the fourth asks only whether that seed finds root in us (the Sower).",
      "claim": "God's word is already at work and will not come back empty; your part is not to manufacture the harvest but to be ground that lets it root.",
      "here": "For anxious sowers — parents, teachers, pastors — who fear their labor falls on hard ground, the move is to trust the seed's own power and the Spirit's fertility: keep scattering, and tend the soil of your own attention rather than counting the yield."
    },
    "subtract": "Three doors, three landings — self-examination (which ground), grace (the reckless Sower), fruitfulness (hearing that yields). Walk through one; the choosing is the focus.",
